# auditd rule for the Cerberus VSOCK-connect detective control
# (docs/vsock-connect-detection.md, Option A). Install to
# /etc/audit/rules.d/61-cerberus-vsock.rules (the numeric prefix follows
# auditd's convention of loading rules.d files in lexical order; 61 places it
# after the distro-default rule files, which is not load-bearing here but
# keeps it out of the way of any numbering convention already in use).
#
# This rule alone is intentionally coarse: it logs every connect(2) from any
# process OTHER than the packaged ssh-cert-api binary. auditd cannot filter on
# socket address family in the rule itself, so cerberus-vsock-watch's auditd
# path (vsockwatch/audit.go) does the real filtering — decoding each
# correlated SYSCALL+SOCKADDR pair and only alerting on a genuine
# AF_VSOCK connect to the enclave's (CID, port). A misconfigured or absent
# rule degrades to "cerberus-vsock-watch has fewer audit log lines to parse",
# not a silent gap in the eBPF detector, which is independent of this rule.
#
# After editing rule files, reload with: augenrules --load (or
# `service auditd restart` on distros without augenrules). Verify with:
#   auditctl -l | grep cerberus_vsock_watch
# cerberus-vsock-watch's TamperWatch (vsockwatch/tamper.go) periodically runs
# this same check and alerts if a previously-observed rule disappears.
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S connect -F exe!=/usr/bin/ssh-cert-api -k cerberus_vsock_watch
-a always,exit -F arch=b32 -S connect -F exe!=/usr/bin/ssh-cert-api -k cerberus_vsock_watch
